# Kit maintainer (this repo only)

This file is **not** `crav1-*` so a folder dump of the library can omit it. Product repos get `.cursor/rules/crav1.mdc` (Cursor) or `.claude/CLAUDE.md` (Claude Code), not this file.

This repo’s custom library uses the **`crav1`** prefix so it is distinct from Cursor built-ins. Skill and agent names stay the same on Claude Code.

There are **three install trees**. Cursor drop-in `.cursor/` is the source of the playbooks. `plugins/crav1/` is the Cursor plugin mirror. `.claude/` is the generated Claude Code drop-in. When you add or change a skill, agent, template, or kit rule, edit `.cursor/` and run `scripts/sync-crav1-plugin.sh`. That refreshes the plugin mirror and regenerates `.claude/`. Do not hand-edit mirrored files under `plugins/crav1/` or anything under `.claude/`. Also update `docs/install.md` and `plugins/crav1/README.md` when how people install or start using the kit changes.

CI (`.github/workflows/drop-in-plugin-sync.yml`) fails the check when the plugin mirror drifts, and when `scripts/sync-crav1-claude.sh --check` exits non-zero because the committed `.claude/` tree does not match a fresh generate. A comment in this rule is not the check.

## Drop-in (project `.cursor/`)

- Skills: `.cursor/skills/crav1/crav1-<short-name>/` with frontmatter `name: crav1-<short-name>` (must match the folder that contains `SKILL.md`). Slash command: `/crav1-<short-name>`. Type `/crav1` to list them.
- Cross-cutting pull-request skills: `/crav1-open-pr` opens one pull request and does not merge. `/crav1-merge-pr` merges one named pull request only when the user explicitly asks in that turn, and only with a merge commit. Do not fold merge into open-pr, complete-task, or review skills.
- Agents: `.cursor/agents/crav1-<name>.md` with `name: crav1-<name>`. Subagent `name:` ends in `-agent` so the Cursor slash picker can tell them apart from skills that share the same stem (`/crav1-architecture-reviewer` stays the skill; `crav1-architecture-reviewer-agent` is the subagent).
- Guild routing: when you add, rename, or remove a skill, agent, or cross-cutting helper, update `docs/guild-routing.md` in the same PR. Update the behavior map in this file in that same PR.
- Agent templates: `.cursor/agent-assets/crav1-<name>/` (not under `.cursor/agents/`).
- Usage rule for consuming repos: `.cursor/rules/crav1.mdc`.

Do not add unprefixed copies at `.cursor/skills/<short-name>/` (name collision).

## Plugin mirror (`plugins/crav1/`)

Cursor plugin layout (install-from-repo / local plugin):

- Manifest: `plugins/crav1/.cursor-plugin/plugin.json`
- Catalog: `.cursor-plugin/marketplace.json` (repo root)
- Skills: `plugins/crav1/skills/crav1-<short-name>/` (same `name:` and `SKILL.md` as drop-in)
- Agents: `plugins/crav1/agents/crav1-<name>.md`
- Agent templates: `plugins/crav1/agent-assets/crav1-<name>/`
- Rules: `plugins/crav1/rules/` (includes `crav1.mdc`; do not put this maintainer file in the plugin)

After editing drop-in files, run `scripts/sync-crav1-plugin.sh` so the plugin tree and `.claude/` match. Do not hand-edit mirrored files only under `plugins/crav1/` or `.claude/` — they will be overwritten. Plugin-only files: `plugins/crav1/.cursor-plugin/plugin.json`, `plugins/crav1/README.md`. There is no Claude Code plugin marketplace in this kit.

## Claude Code drop-in (generated `.claude/`)

`scripts/sync-crav1-plugin.sh` calls `scripts/sync-crav1-claude.sh`, which writes `.claude/` from `.cursor/`. `scripts/sync-crav1-claude.sh --check` diffs a fresh generate against the committed tree and exits non-zero on drift. The same check runs in CI.

Path rewrite only: `.cursor/skills/crav1/<name>/` → `.claude/skills/<name>/`, `.cursor/agents/` → `.claude/agents/`, `.cursor/agent-assets/` → `.claude/agent-assets/`. Commit-style rules rewrite `.cursor/rules/draft-commit-*.mdc` to `.claude/rules/draft-commit-*.md`. The transfer data policy rewrites `.cursor/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.mdc` to `.claude/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.md`. The source skill text names Cursor and Claude Code; the generator rewrites the Cursor paths in the Claude copy.

Hand-written host differences inside that generator, not a second copy of every `SKILL.md`:

- Agent frontmatter. `model: inherit` is omitted (Claude Code’s default). `readonly: true` becomes `tools: Read, Grep, Glob`. Writers omit `tools` so they can edit.
- `.cursor/rules/crav1.mdc` becomes `.claude/CLAUDE.md` (the short project instruction, not the README).
- Commit-style assets drop Cursor `alwaysApply` frontmatter so `.claude/rules/*.md` loads at session start. The transfer data policy asset in `crav1-add-to-spec` gets the same drop.

`.claude/agent-assets/` is not a directory Claude Code loads on its own. Prompts read those files. Do not put them under `.claude/agents/` (that tree is scanned as subagents). Do not copy this maintainer file into `.claude/`.

## Self-contained templates

When you **create or update** a crav1 skill or subagent that reads or writes spec artifacts (`spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, `adr.md`, `diagrams.md`, `verify.md`, `fix-log.md`) or landscape artifacts (`docs/system/`):

1. Put a copy of each template in **that skill’s** `assets/` (and the matching `agent-assets/crav1-<agent-name>/` on both trees).
2. Point the `SKILL.md` / agent prompt at **those** copies, not only `docs/specs/_template/`. Mention drop-in `.cursor/…` **and** plugin sibling paths when a file lives outside this skill. The Claude generator rewrites the `.cursor/` paths.
3. Keep `docs/specs/_template/` and `docs/system/_template/` in sync with the matching `assets/` copies. If you change a template, update **every** copy.
4. Do **not** put template markdown under `.cursor/agents/` or `plugins/crav1/agents/` except the agent prompt file.
5. Format recipes live in the skill’s `references/` (see `crav1-export-spec` / `crav1-ideas-to-spec`).

Skills that do not use those files do not need empty `assets/` folders.

Optional Azure Boards id file: `docs/specs/_template/work-item.md` is the HTML-comment starter only. Do not copy that template into skill `assets/`, and do not copy the comment into a slug. Commit and pull-request skills read `docs/specs/<slug>/work-item.md` when it exists. The file holds one id per line, with a milestone or slice label when one is recorded. A single unlabeled line is the old format and is still read. The mention picks the line whose label matches the slice being worked, and asks when that is unclear. `/crav1-spark-to-spec`, `/crav1-ideas-to-spec`, and `/crav1-intake-to-specs` may write a line when the user opts in on Azure Repos (`crav1-draft-commit-message` `references/work-item-offer.md`). They do not write the file when the user skips. Creating a Feature is `/crav1-specs-to-ado`.

## Install docs

Pre-install: `docs/install.md`. Post-plugin-install: `plugins/crav1/README.md`. Kit playbook: `README.md`, `docs/from-nothing.md`, `docs/from-ideas.md`, `docs/from-intake.md`.

## Behavior map

This map covers main. Read it before answering how a skill behaves. Lane ownership stays in `docs/guild-routing.md`. One line per skill: what it reads, what it writes, what it leaves alone, who calls it, and which skill or agent it calls. A second line is a real exception. An agent is listed only when it runs a skill. Naming a next command is not a call.

Starter options, when the user asks for startup options: `/crav1-spark-to-spec`, `/crav1-ideas-to-spec`, `/crav1-intake-to-specs`, `/crav1-match-to-specs`, `/crav1-repos-to-spec`, `/crav1-environment-read`, and `/crav1-fix-bug`. That ask names the list. It does not run a skill and does not move work into a lane. Every other skill is a later skill. Each starter still follows the rules it already has.

- `/crav1-spark-to-spec` — Reads the spark and the repo. Writes `docs/specs/<slug>/spec.md`, a thin `docs/system/` including `glossary.md` when that folder is missing, otherwise one index row plus a missing glossary or new glossary rows, and `work-item.md` when the user opts in on Azure Repos. A `repos.md` it writes includes a `Synced at` column and leaves that cell empty. When this run read only that slice's code, writes or updates `Synced at: <repo> <branch>@<short sha>, <date>` on that slice's `spec.md` and does not touch the repo column. Leaves application code and an existing landscape alone. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-feature-branch` when the repo already has commits.
  Exception: extend edits that spec and adds no second index row.
- `/crav1-ideas-to-spec` — Reads one unstructured pile and the repo. Writes `spec.md`, `diagrams.md`, slice ADRs, the chosen `export/`, optional `notes.md`, the same landscape or index-and-glossary result as spark, and `work-item.md` on an Azure Repos opt-in. Where it reads a whole repo, sets that repo's `Synced at` cell and touches only those repos. Where it reads only one slice, leaves that column alone and writes or updates `Synced at: <repo> <branch>@<short sha>, <date>` on that slice's `spec.md`. Leaves application code and the rest of an existing landscape alone. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-feature-branch` when the repo already has commits.
  Exception: two or more v0 slices or repos writes nothing and points at `/crav1-intake-to-specs`.
- `/crav1-intake-to-specs` — Reads the intake files. Writes `docs/system/` including `glossary.md` (a missing glossary and new rows when the folder already exists), one `docs/specs/<slug>/` per confirmed v0 feature, the landscape index, `work-item.md` on an Azure Repos opt-in, and `docs/sources/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>/` when an intake file it quotes is an imported source, following `crav1-add-to-spec` `references/sources.md`. That check may write the transfer data policy after a yes. Cursor: `.cursor/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.mdc`. Claude Code project: `.claude/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.md`. It never writes a secrets choice into that file. Where it reads a whole repo, sets that repo's `Synced at` cell and touches only those repos. Where it reads only one slice, leaves that column alone and writes or updates `Synced at: <repo> <branch>@<short sha>, <date>` on that slice's `spec.md`. Leaves application code, `plan.md`, and later slices alone. Does not commit. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-feature-branch` when the repo already has commits.
  Exception: prompts `/crav1-finalize-commit` and does not run it.
- `/crav1-match-to-specs` — Reads the repos and the dump. Writes `docs/system/` in the destination the user picks (gaps only when it exists, including a missing glossary and new rows) and one `docs/specs/<slug>/spec.md` per confirmed slice, including not in the code, then index rows. When a dump file it quotes is an imported source, also writes `docs/sources/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>/`, following `crav1-add-to-spec` `references/sources.md`. That check may write the transfer data policy after a yes. Cursor: `.cursor/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.mdc`. Claude Code project: `.claude/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.md`. It never writes a secrets choice into that file. A `repos.md` it writes includes a `Synced at` column and leaves that cell empty. It does not set or clear an existing cell. Leaves application code, `plan.md`, `work-item.md`, and the rest of an existing landscape alone. Does not commit. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-feature-branch` in that destination when it already has commits.
  Exception: a slice that is not in the code stays thin and is not planned here.
- `/crav1-match-dump-to-specs` — Reads the dump and existing spec folders, skipping `_template`. Writes confirmed new quotes into the matching `spec.md` files, and a missing `glossary.md` or new glossary rows only when `docs/system/` already exists. When a confirmed quote comes from an imported source, also writes `docs/sources/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>/`, following `crav1-add-to-spec` `references/sources.md`. That check may write the transfer data policy after a yes. Cursor: `.cursor/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.mdc`. Claude Code project: `.claude/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.md`. It never writes a secrets choice into that file. A listed `repos.md` edit keeps `Synced at` and does not set it. Leaves leftovers, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, and other landscape files alone unless the user picks apply. Does not commit. Called by the user. Calls no skill.
  Exception: creates no slug and seeds no `docs/system/`.
- `/crav1-code-into-specs` — Reads a named commit, range, or branch diff, and existing spec folders. Writes quotes of what belongs into the matching `spec.md` files, and the same glossary gap as match-dump. When the read is a whole repo, sets that repo's `Synced at` cell and touches only those repos. When the read is one slice, leaves that column alone and writes or updates `Synced at: <repo> <branch>@<short sha>, <date>` on that slice's `spec.md`. Leaves leftovers, application code, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, and other landscape files alone unless the user picks apply. Called by the user. Calls no skill.
  Exception: creates no slug, seeds no `docs/system/`, and does not watch the repo.
- `/crav1-add-to-spec` — Reads the new information and one existing `spec.md`. Writes quotes into that file, and listed edits to `docs/system/` or another spec only when the user picks apply, including a new row on an existing glossary. When that information is an imported source, also writes `docs/sources/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>/` following this skill’s `references/sources.md`, and `Source:` and `Trace:` lines that link to that folder. That check may write the transfer data policy after a yes. Cursor: `.cursor/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.mdc`. Claude Code project: `.claude/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.md`. It never writes a secrets choice into that file. A meeting handoff that already wrote the folder only adds those lines. A listed `repos.md` edit keeps `Synced at` and does not set it. Leaves `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, and a missing `glossary.md` alone. Does not commit. Called by the user, by `/crav1-environment-read` for a confirmed Match section, by `/crav1-suggest-tests-for-code` for one kept or dismissed suggestion, by `/crav1-meeting-to-specs` for one kept addition, by `/crav1-specs-to-ado` for a preview change, and by `/crav1-ado-to-specs` for one kept change or one answer. Calls no skill.
  Exception: creates no slug and no `docs/system/`. The environment-read and suggest-tests-for-code handoffs ask once at the end of the run, not per item, whether to run `/crav1-keep-current`, and do not run it. The specs-to-ado and ado-to-specs handoffs do not ask.
- `/crav1-architecture-reviewer` — Reads `spec.md`, `diagrams.md`, `adr/`, and `export/` when those exist. Writes nothing. Leaves the spec and code alone. Called by the user. Calls no skill.
  Exception: the parent delegates the critique and does not review in its own voice. It names `/crav1-tighten-spec` and `/crav1-security-review` and does not run them.
- `/crav1-tighten-spec` — Reads `spec.md`, diagrams, ADRs, reviewer findings already in the chat, and, when the slice is partial or `## In the code` names paths, the committed code on the repo and branch that spec names. Writes only the picked issue in `spec.md` and, when that pick says so, the named diagram, ADR, or export. Writes a branch onto the spec only after a yes. Leaves application code and unpicked issues alone. A spec with no code behind it skips that read. Called by the user. Calls no skill.
  Exception: a different open repo or branch, a missing branch, a stub, or a spec too thin for that read stops and does not tighten. `suggest` does not patch. A disagreement with the code is called out and the spec is not rewritten to match the code. Finish, add a test, and leave are not choices here.
- `/crav1-resolve-questions` — Reads Open questions and unresolved assumptions in `spec.md`. Writes only the picked question into the spec, a constraint, a non-goal, or an ADR. Leaves application code and kept-open questions alone. Called by the user. Calls no skill.
- `/crav1-plan-from-spec` — Reads the accepted spec, diagrams, ADRs, exports, kept security findings already written, and, when the spec is partial or `## In the code` names paths, that same committed repo and branch before any task is written. Writes `plan.md` and `tasks.md`, and matching OpenSpec export files when those already exist. A built piece that already matches v0 is marked already there and gets no rebuild task. A piece the user leaves gets one line in the plan and no task. Leaves `spec.md`, application code, and any linter install alone. Called by the user. Calls no skill.
  Exception: when `## Linter` says this repo has no linter for the code the tasks will touch, it stops before Build until the user chooses. A line under `## Dismissed test suggestions` is not a task. A kept test suggestion that is already an acceptance line maps to a task. A gap or a disagreement is finish, add a verify test, or leave before any task exists. A later run does not turn a left piece into work unless the user says so.
- `/crav1-review-plan` — Reads `spec.md`, `plan.md`, and `tasks.md`. Writes nothing. Leaves the plan and code alone. Called by the user. Calls no skill.
  Exception: the parent delegates the critique and does not review in its own voice. It names `/crav1-tighten-plan` and does not run it. A piece marked already there or left as committed is not a missing task.
- `/crav1-tighten-plan` — Reads `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, `spec.md`, and plan-review findings already in the chat. Writes only the picked plan issue in `plan.md` or `tasks.md`. Leaves `spec.md` and application code alone. Called by the user. Calls no skill.
  Exception: a spec-tagged finding is not patched here.
- `/crav1-implement-task` — Reads one `T#` in `tasks.md`, plus `plan.md` and `spec.md`. Writes the code and the checks that row names, and marks the row `[x]` only when that verify passes. Leaves `spec.md` and other tasks alone. Called by the user and by `crav1-complete-task-agent`. Calls `/crav1-feature-branch` before code.
  Exception: stops with no code when `## Linter` says none and the user has not chosen to go on. It does not ask to run `/crav1-keep-current`.
- `/crav1-complete-task` — Reads one `T#` and the persist rule. Writes the commit-style rule when it is missing. Leaves product code, `spec.md`, push, and the pull request alone in this chat. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-feature-branch` and `crav1-complete-task-agent`.
  Exception: it does not ask to run `/crav1-keep-current`.
- `/crav1-complete-tasks` — Reads the `T#` queue on one spec. Writes the commit-style rule once when it is missing. Leaves product code, commits, `spec.md`, push, and the pull request alone in this chat. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-feature-branch` and `crav1-complete-task-agent` once per `T#`.
- `/crav1-complete-features` — Reads ready spec folders already on the default branch. Writes the commit-style rule once when it is missing, and a `feat/<slug>` checkout from default per slug. Leaves product code, commits, `spec.md`, push, and the pull request alone in this chat. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-feature-branch` for that checkout, without re-prompting, and `crav1-complete-task-agent` one `T#` at a time.
  Exception: one ready slug stops and points at `/crav1-complete-tasks` without running it. When a slice is marked done or retired, it asks once whether to run `/crav1-keep-current` and does not run it.
- `crav1-complete-task-agent` — Reads one `T#` (`spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`). Writes that task’s code, `tasks.md`, `verify.md`, `fix-log.md` when a fix runs, and the commits. Leaves `spec.md`, other `T#`s, push, and the pull request alone. Called by `/crav1-complete-task`, `/crav1-complete-tasks`, and `/crav1-complete-features`. Calls `/crav1-implement-task`, `/crav1-verify-spec`, `/crav1-fix-from-verify`, and `/crav1-finalize-commit`.
- `/crav1-verify-spec` — Reads `spec.md`, `tasks.md`, `plan.md`, and the running tree. Writes `verify.md`. Leaves `spec.md` and product code alone. Called by the user and by `crav1-complete-task-agent`. Calls no skill.
  Exception: does not run `/crav1-fix-bug` or `/crav1-review-pr`.
- `/crav1-fix-from-verify` — Reads `verify.md` and the spec folder. Writes the smallest in-spec fix, an appended `fix-log.md`, and that one `verify.md` row when the file exists. Leaves `spec.md` and unimplemented tasks alone. Called by the user and by `crav1-complete-task-agent`. Calls no skill.
  Exception: does not run `/crav1-fix-bug`.
- `/crav1-fix-live` — Reads the same queue as `/crav1-fix-from-verify`. Writes what that skill writes. Leaves `spec.md` alone. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-fix-from-verify`.
  Exception: prefers live or untested wiring rows. When the fix changes how the parts connect, it asks once whether to run `/crav1-keep-current` and does not run it.
- `/crav1-feature-branch` — Reads the current branch, the default branch, and whether the tree is dirty. Writes a local checkout of `feat/<slug>` or `spec/<slug>` after the user picks. Leaves commits, push, and the pull request alone. Called by the user, `/crav1-spark-to-spec`, `/crav1-ideas-to-spec`, `/crav1-intake-to-specs`, `/crav1-match-to-specs`, `/crav1-implement-task`, `/crav1-complete-task`, `/crav1-complete-tasks`, and `/crav1-complete-features`. Calls no skill.
  Exception: skips the prompt when there is no repo, no commits, or the branch is already right. A dirty tree does not checkout.
- `/crav1-security-review` — Reads `docs/system/` or a spec that already describes the design, and the change when that is the target. Writes kept findings to `docs/system/security.md` or a Security section on that spec. Leaves code, `plan.md`, and commits alone. Called by the user. Calls no skill.
  Exception: spark, plan, and verify do not run it. `/crav1-architecture-reviewer` may name it and does not run it.
- `/crav1-fix-bug` — Reads only the bug the user named and a spec already in front of them. Writes nothing. Leaves code, specs, plans, `verify.md`, and `work-item.md` alone. Called by the user. Calls no skill.
  Exception: no bug named, or not a real bug, stops with no lane. Spark, specify, plan, and verify do not run it. When the bug changes how the parts connect, it asks once whether to run `/crav1-keep-current` and does not run it.
- `/crav1-exploratory-test` — Reads a built slice, `spec.md`, and `plan.md`. Writes bugs in `docs/specs/<slug>/explore.md`. Leaves `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, `verify.md`, application code, and the checks the plan already named alone. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-fix-bug` only when the user says so in that turn.
  Exception: no build of the slice stops with nothing written. The second pass stops at `matched the first pass`, or sooner at `the user said stop`. It does not start Specify, Plan, or Build. It is not a starter option.
- `/crav1-suggest-tests-for-code` — Reads one repo or one area of code that already exists, the tests with that code, and the existing spec. Writes nothing itself. Leaves tests, application code, `plan.md`, and `tasks.md` alone. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-add-to-spec` for one kept suggestion and for one dismissed suggestion.
  Exception: the whole system, no code pointed at, or no existing spec stops with no list. Leave writes nothing and is offered again. Dismiss is not offered again. It does not start Specify, Plan, or Build. Plan turns a kept suggestion into a task. It is not a starter option.
- `/crav1-meeting-to-specs` — Reads the minutes, transcript, email, or chat after the command, and existing `docs/specs/<slug>/` folders, skipping `_template`. For an item that matches a slice, also reads that slice’s `spec.md` (including `## Match`), `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, and `verify.md` when present, and the code that spec or plan points to only when those files do not settle the build state. When a kept addition imports a source, writes `docs/sources/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>/` following `crav1-add-to-spec` `references/sources.md`, and does not write the spec. That check may write the transfer data policy after a yes. Cursor: `.cursor/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.mdc`. Claude Code project: `.claude/rules/crav1-transfer-data-policy.md`. It never writes a secrets choice into that file. Does not commit. Leaves `docs/system/`, specs, marks, and dismissals alone. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-add-to-spec` for one kept addition.
  Exception: no input stops and asks. The kind and the date come from the input, one of each for the run. An unclear kind, or no date, is one question. Dismiss is this run only and is not stored. A kept new feature or bug only names the next command. A build-state line on a matched item is informational. It is not quote text. It does not start Specify, Plan, or Build. It is not a starter option.
- `/crav1-feature-candidates` — Reads `docs/system/landscape.md` (the spec index, Ideas, Way of working, and code with no slice), `docs/system/repos.md`, the glossary, every `docs/specs/*/spec.md` (milestones, slices, open questions, and actions), `plan.md`, `tasks.md`, and `verify.md` where present, `docs/research/` and `docs/sources/` with each fact labelled by its source, each spec’s `work-item.md`, and the landscape “No spec yet in ADO” table. Writes nothing unless the user asks for a file. Leaves specs, Azure Boards, and commits alone. Called by the user and by `/crav1-specs-to-ado`. Calls no skill.
  Exception: a dated milestone or slice is one candidate. An undated spec with open work is one candidate. A spec that holds only open questions is marked questions only. An item with no spec is marked no spec yet. Done or retired with nothing open is skipped. A target date is given only when the docs state it. It does not start Specify, Plan, or Build. It is not a starter option.
- `/crav1-specs-to-ado` — Reads the org and project from `docs/environments/marks.md` or Azure DevOps URLs in `docs/system/repos.md`, checks `az` including the default install paths, checks that the project has the Feature work item type, reads the Feature fields before the preview, and reads the candidate list. When the type has Acceptance Criteria, the checks go there. When it does not, the checks go in the Description as an Acceptance checks list and that field is not sent. Writes a Feature only after the user says yes. After a Create, writing the new id into that spec’s `work-item.md` with its milestone or slice label, or a row on the landscape “No spec yet in ADO” table when the item has no spec, is required. Leaves the preview unedited, Iteration and Priority blank, and the specs repo out of anything sent. Called by the user. Calls `/crav1-feature-candidates`, and `/crav1-add-to-spec` when a preview change belongs in the spec.
